// TEST: Group Bucket Inline Argument (rejects)
// ERROR: BL3007
// ERROR-CONTAINS: requires the BARE NAME of a `group_keys(...)` binding
// `group_bucket` takes the NAME of a group_keys binding, never an expression
// that produces one.
//
// A grouping is not a first-class value. Its entire runtime state lives in C++
// locals named after the binding -- `<gk>__ngroups`, `<gk>__offsets`,
// `<gk>__perm` (the ABI in genGroupKeysBinding) -- and same-gk co-iteration is
// discharged on expression NAME IDENTITY, not on the type. An inline
// `group_keys(...)` has no binding name for those suffixes to hang off.
//
// Refused at TYPECHECK with an explanation. Left to codegen this would emit C++
// naming an undeclared symbol, i.e. a raw g++ error for what is a language
// rule, or worse a silent miscompile.
let keys = [0, 1, 0, 1]
let b = group_bucket(group_keys(keys))
// The diagnostic says what to write instead: bind the keys to a name first,
// then pass that name to group_bucket.
